# --- why TypeScript dominates this space ---
#
# 1. teams already knew it — Anthropic, OpenAI, and Google have large
#    TypeScript/JS engineering orgs. ship in the language your team
#    already writes at scale: faster iteration, more contributors, smaller
#    context switch. you don't pick the *best* language; you pick the one
#    your team will actually ship in.
#
# 2. ecosystem is enormous — npm has everything a coding agent needs:
#    terminal UI (ink, blessed, chalk), HTTP clients, YAML/JSON parsing,
#    file system ops, streaming, MCP integration. assemble, don't build.
#
# 3. streaming LLM output is natural — Node.js async/event-driven model
#    is a genuinely good fit for streaming token-by-token LLM output.
#    what's awkward in a synchronous language is idiomatic in Node.js.
#
# 4. cross-platform without effort — Node.js runs on macOS, Linux, and
#    Windows with no platform-specific build toolchain. Rust/Go need a
#    compiled binary per target; a TypeScript CLI ships via npm and works
#    everywhere immediately.
#
# 5. prototyping speed — the space is moving fast; labs ship and rewrite
#    every few months. TypeScript moves faster than Rust or C++. the cost
#    (startup latency, runtime dep) is acceptable when iteration speed
#    matters more than binary size.
#
# tradeoffs accepted:
# - requires Node.js (not a single static binary)
# - slower cold-start vs. Go / Rust
# - higher memory footprint
#
# why Rust is gaining ground:
# once a tool matures and the architecture stabilizes, performance
# arguments start to matter. Codex's rewrite (TypeScript → Rust) is the
# clearest example — TypeScript to get to market, Rust once the design
# settled. expect others to follow the same arc.


