# --- aws bedrock integration ---
#
# bedrock lets you run models through AWS infrastructure — billing stays
# in AWS, IAM controls access, no separate API keys to rotate. good fit
# for teams already AWS-primary.
#
# tools with confirmed bedrock support:
#
# Claude Code      configure via ANTHROPIC_API_URL pointing to Bedrock endpoint
# Codex CLI        OpenAI + Codex models available on Bedrock (Apr 2026)
# Aider            aider --model bedrock/anthropic.claude-<model-id>
#                  uses standard AWS credential chain
# OpenCode         AWS provider built in; supports AWS_PROFILE,
#                  AWS_ROLE_ARN, AWS_WEB_IDENTITY_TOKEN_FILE (K8s/OIDC)
# Amazon Q         native — it is AWS
#
# auth patterns supported (OpenCode / Aider):
#   AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID + AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY  (basic)
#   AWS_PROFILE                                 (named profile)
#   AWS_WEB_IDENTITY_TOKEN_FILE + AWS_ROLE_ARN  (K8s / OIDC — preferred)
#   AWS_BEARER_TOKEN_BEDROCK                    (direct bearer token)
#
# AWS published a sample repo for OpenCode + Bedrock:
# https://github.com/aws-samples/sample-opencode-with-bedrock
#
# the OIDC / no-long-lived-creds pattern works cleanly with OpenCode —
# use AWS_WEB_IDENTITY_TOKEN_FILE + AWS_ROLE_ARN for K8s environments.


# --- ollama (local model) support ---
#
# ollama exposes an OpenAI-compatible REST endpoint on localhost:11434,
# so any tool that speaks the OpenAI API format can point at it and run
# local models with no API costs, no rate limits, and zero data leaving
# your machine.
#
# key unlock: Ollama 0.22.1 (Apr 2026) added native Anthropic API
# compatibility — Claude Code can now talk directly to Ollama without
# a proxy or translation layer.
#
# tool              ollama support   notes
# ──────────────── ──────────────── ────────────────────────────────────
# Claude Code       ✅ native        Ollama 0.22.1+ (Apr 2026); Anthropic
#                                    API format, no proxy needed
# Codex CLI         ✅ native        OSS mode + Ollama 0.24 (May 2026)
# Aider             ✅ native        long-standing; aider --model ollama/<model>
# OpenCode          ✅ native        full agentic workflows; 75+ providers
# Goose             ✅ native        OpenAI-compatible endpoint support
# Copilot CLI       ✅               added local model support Apr 7, 2026
# Gemini CLI        ⚠️  via proxy    needs OpenAI-compatible proxy layer
# Grok Build        ❌               subscription-gated; no local support
# Amazon Q          ❌               AWS-hosted only
#
# best fully-local setups:
#   Aider + Ollama      most mature, battle-tested
#   OpenCode + Ollama   best TUI, broadest provider config
#   Claude Code + Ollama  native Anthropic format since Apr 2026
#
# 200+ models available via Ollama including Qwen3, DeepSeek V3,
# Llama, Gemma, Mistral, Kimi K2, GLM, and more.


# --- us government access — the parallel with china ---
#
# the parallel is real. US companies can also be compelled to share
# your data with the US government:
#
# CLOUD Act (2018)      US companies must provide stored data to law
#                       enforcement / intelligence on a valid legal order,
#                       even if data is stored overseas. no user notification
#                       required.
# FISA Section 702      warrantless collection of communications from
#                       non-US persons on US-hosted infrastructure.
#                       renewed through 2026.
# NSLs                  FBI can compel disclosure with a gag order — the
#                       company cannot tell you it happened.
# EO 12333              broad signals intelligence collection authority
#                       outside of FISA.
#
# Anthropic, OpenAI, Google, and xAI are all subject to the above.
#
# where the parallel holds:
# - both can be legally compelled to hand over your data
# - neither has to notify you when it happens
# - both represent a nation-state with intelligence interests
#
# where the parallel breaks:
# - transparency  — US companies publish transparency reports and can
#                   challenge orders in court. Chinese companies have
#                   no equivalent right to resist.
# - rule of law   — US orders require judicial oversight (warrants,
#                   court orders). China's intelligence law has no
#                   independent judicial check.
# - scope         — Chinese intelligence law applies to all Chinese
#                   companies with no exceptions. US legal process is
#                   theoretically targeted, not bulk.
# - adversarial   — the US and China represent different threat models
#                   for most users; who is interested in your code matters.
#
# the honest take:
# no major cloud AI provider — US or Chinese — offers ironclad protection
# from government access. meaningful differences are: (1) which government,
# (2) what legal recourse exists, (3) whether the company can fight it.
# US companies can challenge orders; Chinese companies legally cannot.
#
# if true data sovereignty matters: local models via Ollama are the only
# real answer. data that never leaves your machine cannot be subpoenaed
# from a third party.


# --- safety & data sovereignty ---
#
# the core risk with chinese-hosted tools:
# China's National Intelligence Law (2017) requires Chinese companies to
# cooperate with state intelligence agencies on request, with no public
# transparency requirement. code sent to a Chinese-hosted model API is
# potentially accessible to the Chinese government. that's the legal
# structure, not speculation.
#
# risk-ranked (chinese tools):
#
# TRAE (ByteDance)   highest  — ByteDance = TikTok parent; active US govt
#                              scrutiny and ban attempts
# Baidu Comate       high     — Baidu Cloud infrastructure; enterprise-targeted
# Kimi Code          high     — MoonshotAI; model runs on Chinese servers
# ZCode / z.ai       high     — Zhipu AI; GLM models on Chinese infrastructure
# DeepSeek           high     — known data exposure incidents; Chinese infra
# Qwen Code          lower*   — model-agnostic; point it at Anthropic/OpenAI
#                              and your code goes to US servers, not Alibaba's
#
# * Qwen Code is the exception: it's open-source (Apache 2.0, auditable)
#   and genuinely model-agnostic. configure it with a US-based API key and
#   the Chinese-built tool never touches Chinese infrastructure.
#
# safest options by category:
#
# us-native:      Claude Code, Codex CLI, Gemini CLI, Grok Build, Amazon Q
# eu-sovereign:   Mistral Vibe CLI — data stays in EU; GDPR-governed,
#                 not subject to Chinese intelligence law
# local / air-gap: Aider + Ollama, OpenCode + Ollama — code never leaves
#                 your machine; zero data exposure risk
#
# general rule:
# - personal / hobby code: Qwen Code pointed at a US API is reasonable
# - sensitive / proprietary code: US or EU tools only
# - chinese-hosted model APIs for sensitive work: hard no


# --- local hardware — what models run well ---
#
# Dell Pro Max with GB10 (NVIDIA GB10 Grace Blackwell Superchip)
# same silicon as the NVIDIA DGX Spark; $6,332 base.
# key spec: 128GB unified LPDDR5X memory @ ~1TB/s shared CPU+GPU.
# 20 ARM cores (10x Cortex-X925 + 10x Cortex-A725), 4TB NVMe SSD.
# runs DGX OS 7.
#
# what 128GB unified memory unlocks:
# most machines need Q4 quantization to fit a 70B model (sacrificing
# quality). with 128GB you run Q8 or near-full precision — meaningfully
# better output quality with no aggressive compression.
#
# model                  fit         notes
# ─────────────────────── ─────────── ──────────────────────────────────
# Llama 3.3 70B           ✅ easy     full precision fits; fast inference
# Qwen2.5 72B             ✅ easy     strong general model
# Qwen2.5-Coder 72B       ✅ easy     best local coding model at this size
# DeepSeek R1 70B         ✅ easy     strong reasoning
# Gemma 3 27B             ✅ fast     headroom to spare
# Phi-4 14B               ✅ fast     punches above weight on coding
# Llama 3.1 405B Q4       ❌ too big  ~200GB needed; Q2 (~100GB) may squeeze
#
# inference speed: ~20–40 tokens/sec on 70B @ Q4/Q8 (DGX Spark benchmarks)
# fast enough for interactive use with an AI code CLI.
#
# best coding models to pull via ollama:
#   ollama run qwen2.5-coder:72b   # best coding model that fits well
#   ollama run deepseek-coder-v2   # strong alternative
#   ollama run llama3.3:70b        # good general + coding
#
